    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"674484\">Jeremy Chen</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/2025.20Thailand.20MO.2C.20P9/near/609301597\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Field</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ZMod</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">POTD_2662</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Prime</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hodd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Odd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ZMod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hB0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hsq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsSquare</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Equiv</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mulLeft₀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hB0</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">IsSquare</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div><br>\n</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Whilst this is mathematically equivalent to the problem as stated, one might imagine that in a high school olympiad there would be perhaps 1 point available for getting this far (e.g. you have assumed B is not a multiple of p in order to state the question the way you've stated it, but this is not explicitly stated in the question and should be deduced from the hypotheses, and similarly squareness of B being the same as what is asked for in the question also needs an undergraduate-level argument)</p>",
        "id": 609311713,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1783626983
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oops, you're right about the conclusion</p>",
        "id": 609315861,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Chen",
        "timestamp": 1783628426
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p><code>B ^ ((p - 1) / 2) = 1</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>oh and I forgot that <code>B ^ (p / 2) = 1</code> works here since Nat division rounds down. However it looks confusing.</p>",
        "id": 609322976,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Chen",
        "timestamp": 1783630612
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm not sure if I should use orbits or cycles. I want to say that all cycles of U have length 2*ord(B), excluding the 1-cycle (p). Then the sum of the cycle lengths is p.<br>\nBut is it better to say the sum of the orbit sizes is p?</p>",
        "id": 609325740,
        "sender_full_name": "Nathan",
        "timestamp": 1783631656
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(btw, this may not hold when ord(B) is odd)</p>",
        "id": 609326051,
        "sender_full_name": "Nathan",
        "timestamp": 1783631782
    },
